<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Profileable, in the benchmark variant and nowhere else.

    ### What this tag does and why it was in the wrong place

    `<profileable android:shell="true" />` lets a shell user — `adb`, Studio's profiler,
    `simpleperf` — attach to a *non-debuggable* build and read its method traces and its heap. It
    is what makes a macrobenchmark run against a release-shaped APK meaningful, and the
    `:benchmark` module cannot measure startup without it.

    It was declared in `src/main`, which merges into every variant, so it shipped in the release
    APK as well: an app on a reader's phone with profiling open to anyone who can reach it over
    ADB. That is not a remote hole — it needs a cable or an authorised debug bridge — but it is a
    map of the running app handed to whoever has one, in a build whose whole point is that the
    internals are stripped.

    Here it merges into `benchmark` only. `initWith(release)` gives that variant R8, resource
    shrinking and the release manifest, so what the benchmark measures is still the shipped
    shape; this one tag is the difference, and it is the difference the tool needs.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <application>
        <profileable android:shell="true" />
    </application>
</manifest>
